  <li>Multiple workshop coordination</li>
  <li>Custom art and craft commissions</li>
  <li>Bespoke landscape integration</li>
</ul>
<p>Design manager's cost observation</p>
<h4>The mixed-tier home is almost always the right budget answer</h4>
<p>Homeowners consistently overestimate what "fully bespoke" costs and underestimate how much of the aesthetic can be captured with a selective approach. A ₹28-lakh selective-bespoke 3BHK (5–7 bespoke pieces, balance semi-custom) delivers 90% of the visual and functional benefit of a ₹65-lakh comprehensive-bespoke 3BHK. Unless the homeowner specifically wants the last 10% and can afford it comfortably, selective is the smarter answer.</p>
<p>21 · Timeline &amp; Sequencing</p>
<h2>How long bespoke actually takes, and how to compress it</h2>
<p>Bespoke work is slower than semi-custom by a factor of 2× to 3×. Understanding this from the start is essential — timelines are one of the top three reasons bespoke projects go badly, not budget.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Phase</th><th>Semi-custom duration</th><th>Bespoke duration</th><th>Why bespoke takes longer</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Design development</td><td>10–14 days</td><td>21–35 days</td><td>Multiple concept iterations, technical drawings, prototype approvals</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Material sourcing</td><td>2–5 days</td><td>10–25 days</td><td>Rare veneers, specialist stone, custom hardware castings</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Manufacturing</td><td>21–28 days</td><td>35–65 days</td><td>Hand-finish coats, single-piece construction, quality inspection</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Finishing and inspection</td><td>3–5 days</td><td>7–14 days</td><td>Multi-coat lacquer curing, hardware fitment, testing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Site installation</td><td>2–4 days per major piece</td><td>3–7 days per major piece</td><td>Precision fitting, on-site adjustments, coordination</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Full 3BHK total</td><td>10–14 weeks</td><td>16–24 weeks</td><td></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Compression strategies that work</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Parallel workflow</strong> — start bespoke pieces at the same time as civil work, not after</li>
  <li><strong>Material pre-sourcing</strong> — commit to material selection at brief stage, not at manufacturing stage</li>
  <li><strong>Multi-workshop routing</strong> — different piece categories to different workshops in parallel, not sequentially</li>
  <li><strong>Prototype approval discipline</strong> — approve prototypes within 48 hours or explicitly release the piece to production without further revision</li>
</ul>
<h3>Compression strategies that don't work</h3>
<ul>
  <li>"Just push the workshop harder" — bespoke workshops accelerate by cutting corners; the outcome is visible</li>
  <li>Skipping the prototype approval stage — creates rework risk that costs more time than it saves</li>
  <li>Ordering in parallel from workshops that share supply chains — capacity constraints kick in</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><span class="callout__title">The most common timeline mistake</span>Homeowners commission bespoke pieces late in the project after civil work is done, expecting installation in 4 weeks. Bespoke pieces need 8–14 weeks from commissioning. The result is either delayed handover or accepting a compromised piece rushed to market. Commission bespoke pieces at design-signoff stage, not at civil-completion stage.</div>
